proposals.hypothesis:
The catalogue has no part that can lower only the moving tip:
- the prismatic jaw opens at most 60 mm (60 teeth), so it would lose apple, gelatin, banana, tomato and tennis;
- the parallelogram rises more (+46 mm at 85 mm with link 90) and lost 9 groups when tried;
- 12 V servos are refused on budget ($68 against $40 left) and don't address the mechanism anyway (514 g needs about 0.41 + 0.77 = 1.18 of the 1.62 N*m);
- the pad parameters trade banana against the wide boxes along one axis, and the 30 deg body sits at the banana-safe end.

The idea is a table-referenced drop pad: the moving pad rides a short vertical slide fixed to the moving jaw and hangs down to the table under its own weight. Its hanging point is set by the table, not by the jaw angle. At wide openings it hangs at its lower stop. The 20 mm of travel matches the brief's +22 mm rise at 85 mm, so it hangs a few mm above the table and the face meets the box side below the top edge. At narrow openings (tip already at -3 mm) the table pushes it up into the jaw with only its few grams of weight, far below the force limit.

The face keeps today's 30 deg tilt, TPU, groove and width, so its orientation is unchanged at every opening. That matters because a plane face touches a sphere where the face's normal passes through the centre, a point set by the face angle and not by the tip height. So the balls, fruit and lying cans keep today's contact points.

During lift the slide is free. A held object stays on the fixed pad by friction: on a small object, mu 0.4 x 20 N = 8 N against at most 0.7 N of weight. On wide objects the pad already sits at its hard stop, so the object's weight goes into the stop.
